Source: netcfgd
Section: net
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Nabeel Sowan <nabeel@vibes.se>
Homepage: https://github.com/netcfgd/netcfgd
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), cargo (>= 1.85), rustc (>= 1.85),
 libncurses-dev, pkgconf,
 qt6-base-dev <pkg.netcfgd.gui>,
# TDE's CMake modules are a package of their own: tde-cmake carries
# TDEMacros, TDEVersion and TDESetupPaths, which is what decides where a
# kpart and a control-centre desktop file go. tdelibs14-trinity-dev brings
# the headers and dcopidl, and depends on libtqtinterface-dev, which is where
# tmoc comes from -- so TQt3 is not named here despite being what this is
# written against.
 cmake <pkg.netcfgd.tde>, tde-cmake <pkg.netcfgd.tde>,
 tdelibs14-trinity-dev <pkg.netcfgd.tde>
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: netcfgd
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
# `wpasupplicant` and a DHCP client are the floor rather than a suggestion.
# Decision 0014 makes wpa_supplicant the wireless backend outright -- netcfgd
# does not implement association and has no fallback that does -- and an
# address on a modern network comes from DHCP. As Suggests, apt installed
# neither, so `apt install netcfgd` on a machine that never had NetworkManager
# produced a daemon that refused every radio with "no wpa_supplicant found"
# and could not take an address. Recommends installs them by default and an
# operator who genuinely wants neither can still say --no-install-recommends.
#
# The rest stay Suggests, and the line between them is whether netcfgd works
# without one: hostapd is for a machine serving an access point, openvpn and
# ppp and radvd are features, and the resolver packages are alternatives to
# each other that only a chosen `dns { mode }` calls for.
#
# netcfgd-nm is a Suggests and deliberately not a Recommends. apt installs
# Recommends by default, so recommending it would put a package whose whole
# purpose is to displace NetworkManager onto every machine that installs
# netcfgd -- and with it a D-Bus policy granting that bus name. It starts
# nothing on install, but the line this package holds everywhere else is that
# installing must not change how the machine's network works, and shipping the
# means to take a bus name is close enough to that line to stay opt-in. A
# Suggests is how somebody finds out it exists.
Recommends: wpasupplicant, dhcpcd-base
Suggests: netcfgd-nm, netcfgd-gui, netcfgd-modem, hostapd, openvpn, ppp, pppoe,
 radvd, dnsmasq-base, unbound, openresolv, systemd-resolved
Description: network configuration daemon that states what it will do
 netcfgd reads one declarative configuration, reports what the machine
 currently looks like, and prints the exact list of operations that would
 make one match the other. Applying is a separate step, and the plan is the
 thing an operator reviews.
 .
 It links no D-Bus, no polkit and no glib, and depends on no system message
 bus. The daemon speaks a newline-delimited JSON protocol over a unix socket.
 .
 Every program in Suggests is optional and none is needed to install, start
 or configure netcfgd. Each is pulled in by one feature: a DHCP lease wants
 dhcpcd, a wifi network wants wpasupplicant, a DNS mode wants whichever
 resolver it names. netcfgd speaks netlink itself, so it needs no ip, iw or
 nft. Prefix delegation wants odhcp6c, which Debian does not package.
 .
 The Qt client is not in this package. It is netcfgd-gui, built from this
 same source under a build profile, so that a plain build of the daemon
 needs no toolkit at all.
 .
 Installing this package configures nothing and starts nothing. The systemd
 unit is installed and left disabled: netcfgd takes over an interface only
 when a configuration names it and an operator applies a plan.

Package: netcfgd-nm
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, netcfgd (= ${binary:Version}),
 dbus-system-bus-common
Description: NetworkManager-compatible D-Bus interface for netcfgd
 netcfgd-nm answers org.freedesktop.NetworkManager from netcfgd, so that
 nmcli, nmtui, nm-applet and plasma-nm show and control what netcfgd is
 actually doing. It is a read-mostly projection of netcfgd's own
 desired-state document plus the wifi flows, not a reimplementation of
 NetworkManager: VPN, modems and the rest are deliberately not projected.
 .
 It is a separate package because it is a separate dependency stack. The
 daemon links no D-Bus, no glib and no polkit and is meant to build and run
 on machines that have none of them; this brings zbus and its transitive
 dependencies, and they stop here.
 .
 Nothing is enabled or started by installing it. Only one process can own
 org.freedesktop.NetworkManager, so starting this stops NetworkManager --
 which is a decision about the whole machine and is left to the operator:
 .
   systemctl enable --now netcfgd netcfgd-nm
 .
 Going back needs no network and touches no package, which is the property
 that matters when netcfgd is what has gone wrong:
 .
   systemctl disable --now netcfgd netcfgd-nm
   systemctl enable --now NetworkManager
 .
 This package deliberately does not Conflict with network-manager in the
 dpkg sense. Both may be installed; only one may run. Removing the
 NetworkManager package to install this one would be the version of the
 switch that cannot be undone by somebody who has just lost their network.
 NetworkManager's own connection profiles are never read or written by
 netcfgd, so it comes back knowing every network it knew.

Package: netcfgd-modem
Architecture: all
# `${source:Version}` and not `${binary:Version}`, which is the rule for an
# arch:all package depending on an arch:any one: a binNMU bumps the binary
# version of netcfgd and not of this, and an exact dependency on the binary
# version would then be unsatisfiable.
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, netcfgd (= ${source:Version})
# `mbimcli` for one of the three helpers and `gpiod` for the SIM-select hook
# example, which is not run by anything as shipped. Neither is needed to
# install this, and netcfgd-modem-at -- the one a board with no MBIM has to
# use -- needs nothing but a serial port.
Suggests: libmbim-utils, gpiod
Description: cellular modem helpers for netcfgd
 The programs that talk to a cellular module on netcfgd's behalf, and the
 data that describes known modules. netcfgd itself drives no modem: it reads
 a file a helper writes and publishes the SIM source and APN a helper reads,
 and the two do not otherwise know about each other.
 .
 Three helpers, because one interface does not fit every module. MBIM through
 mbimcli where glib is already paid for; MBIM through umbim for OpenWrt-class
 boxes where it is not; and AT commands over a serial port, for the large
 class of modules that offer neither MBIM nor QMI and appear only as a tty.
 Which one a machine needs is a property of its module rather than a choice,
 and /usr/share/netcfgd/modem-quirks says which is which for the modules
 somebody has measured.
 .
 A udev rule gives the AT port a stable name and asks ModemManager to leave
 it alone. Both matter on a module that is reset in ordinary operation --
 switching SIM is a modem reset -- because the ttyUSB number moves, and
 because ModemManager takes both AT ports once the modem registers, which is
 exactly when the PDP context first has an APN and resolvers worth reading.
 .
 Architecture: all. Every file is a shell script or text; the work is done by
 the module, by mbimcli or umbim, and by netcfgd.
 .
 Installing this package configures nothing and starts nothing. The systemd
 unit is a template, installed and left disabled: which interface a module
 presents is the operator's to say.
 .
 It ships two SIM-select hook examples and runs neither. netcfgd chooses
 which SIM source it wants and publishes that; moving the mux means driving a
 line that belongs to the board, and netcfgd has no GPIO. One example is
 filled in for a mux on a GPIO expander driven with gpiod, and one is a bare
 frame for a mux that is not GPIO at all.

Package: netcfgd-gui
Architecture: any
Build-Profiles: <pkg.netcfgd.gui>
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, netcfgd (= ${binary:Version})
Description: desktop client and tray applet for netcfgd
 A Qt Widgets client for netcfgd: what the machine's network looks like, what
 the configuration says, what applying it would change, and the wifi networks
 in range. It talks to netcfgd over the same unix socket `ncfg` uses and holds
 no authority of its own, so what it can do is whatever the control policy
 says the person running it may do.
 .
 It is behind a build profile rather than in a source package of its own. The
 daemon must build on machines with no toolkit at all, so qt6-base-dev cannot
 be an unconditional build dependency -- and a separate source package, which
 is the usual answer to that, would have to duplicate the shared C client
 under client/ that both this and the daemon's own conformance gate build.
 One copy of that, and one version number, is worth more than the separation.
 .
 Build it with:
 .
   DEB_BUILD_PROFILES=pkg.netcfgd.gui dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc
 .
 or `make deb-gui`, which does the same. A plain build produces the daemon
 and needs no Qt.

Package: netcfgd-trinity
Architecture: any
Section: tde
Build-Profiles: <pkg.netcfgd.tde>
# `netcfgd-gui` is where both terminal launchers actually live: netcfgd-tui is
# a symlink to that binary, which reads argv[0] and renders on a character
# grid. Three of the four launchers are therefore that package, and a
# Recommends installs it by default while leaving a machine that wants only
# the control module and the profile switcher able to decline. It is not a
# Depends because the module, the status line and profile switching all work
# without it, over the socket, through this package's own code.
#
# `tdebase-trinity-bin` for tdesu, which only the root terminal entry uses.
# A TDE desktop has it; a machine that installed tdelibs for something else
# may not, and the other three entries do not care.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, netcfgd (= ${binary:Version})
Recommends: netcfgd-gui, tdebase-trinity-bin
Description: Trinity control module and tray applet for netcfgd
 A TDE Control Center module and a system tray monitor for netcfgd, both
 TQt3, both speaking the same unix socket `ncfg` uses. The tray shows what
 the daemon is doing, switches between configuration profiles, and starts
 the desktop client, the terminal client, or the terminal client as root.
 .
 It is a second front end rather than the Qt one repackaged, and the reason
 is not the toolkit APIs -- TQt3 is Qt 3 with the types renamed and reads
 like Qt Widgets throughout. A control-centre module is a shared library
 loaded into a host process that is already running TQt3, and the factory
 contract hands back a TDECModule. Two Qt-family toolkits cannot share one
 process's event loop, X connection and metaobject system, so the Qt window
 could not be that object however it were packaged.
 .
 Nothing here reimplements the protocol: both artifacts link the same C
 client library under client/ that the Qt window and the daemon's own
 conformance gate build, so the permission tiers are read from the daemon
 rather than guessed.
 .
 Switching a profile asks first. The daemon reconciles as soon as the
 selection is written, so there is no later step at which anybody gets to
 look, and a profile switch can take the link down on a machine being
 administered over it.
 .
 It is behind a build profile for the same reason netcfgd-gui is, and more
 so: the daemon must build on machines with no toolkit at all, and a TDE
 desktop is a stronger assumption than a Qt one. Build it with:
 .
   DEB_BUILD_PROFILES=pkg.netcfgd.tde dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc
 .
 or `make deb-tde`. A plain build produces the daemon and needs neither.
 .
 It installs a TDE autostart entry, so the tray appears at the next login.
 That is conditional on `netcfgdrc:General:Autostart` and the control module
 has the checkbox for it -- TDE's own mechanism, the one tdepowersave and
 korgac use. The default is true, which is the one place a package built
 from this source does start something: a tray holds no authority of its
 own and changes nothing about the machine's network, so the sentence the
 other packages carry is narrowed here rather than quietly dropped.
 .
 Installing this package configures nothing, and starts no daemon.
